  "working": "Given equilibrium: 2 C5H6(g) ⇌ C10H12(g). Linear regression of ln K vs 1/T yields slope = 10400 K and intercept = -17.1. Thus ln K = (10400 K)(1/T) - 17.1, where K is the equilibrium constant (presumably Kp). Initial pure cyclopentadiene gas at P0 = 1.38 bar in evacuated rigid reactor. Let initial moles of C5H6 = n0. At equilibrium with conversion X = 0.90, moles: C5H6 = n0(1-X), C10H12 = n0 X. Partial pressures proportional to moles (same volume). So Kp = P_C10H12 / (P_C5H6)^2 = (n0 X / n0) P0 / ((n0(1-X)/n0) P0)^2 = X P0 / (1-X)^2 P0^2 = X / [(1-X)^2 P0]. Thus Kp = 0.90 / (0.10^2 * 1.38) = 0.90 / (0.0138) ≈ 65.2 bar. Assuming the regression uses Kp (or K relative to standard pressure), we set ln K ≈ ln Kp = ln(65.2) ≈ 4.18. Solve 4.18 = 10400/T - 17.1 → 10400/T = 21.28 → T = 10400 / 21.28 ≈ 489.6 K. Rounded to three significant figures: 490 K.",
  "answer": "[TYPE: thermochemistry] [CALC_VALUE: 490] [CALC_UNIT: K] [SUBSTANCE: null]",
  "objection": null
}

